From e9610d7f906b14d573a3011f70a5b04de7643ede Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DogukanDG <dagtekindogukan1@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 13:45:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/8] Add a resource eligibility plugin

Scylla can express which resources exist and how many, or which resources
may perform which activity, but not both. A list of resources on an activity
means all of them are required at once, so representing alternatives as a
pool per activity duplicates every resource used by more than one: on BPIC
2012 that raised total capacity from 47 to 191. Putting every resource in a
single shared pool keeps capacity right but lets every activity draw on all
47, which removes most of the queueing -- measured against Prosimos on the
same model, mean waiting time was 422 s against 3914 s.

This plugin takes over resource assignment so both hold at once. The pool
still declares the instances and their calendars; the plugin restricts each
activity to the resources the model lists for it, and picks the least
recently used among those that are free.

Selection keeps Scylla's own rule rather than imitating another engine's.
With two resources on one activity at 10 s and 100 s, both always available,
Scylla and Prosimos each give the fast one 91% of executions, so there was
nothing to reconcile.

QueueManager gains pollAvailable(typeId, wanted, now) and returnResources().
A plugin replacing assignment has to see which instances are free and take
one, and the queue map is private -- deliberately, since the bookkeeping
belongs there. These keep it there: unavailable and unwanted instances go
back before the call returns, and the caller hands back what it does not use.
Existing behaviour is untouched; nothing calls them unless a plugin does.

Activities with no <eligibleResources> fall through to Scylla's own
assignment, so a configuration without the element behaves as before.
